y necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations. ", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"content": "engenn5le18ob04re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear when preparing food with friends.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Review - Long Vowel Sounds", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "There are rules for the long vowel sound.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Explanation\\t\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Example\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Rule 1\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" When a word has <span class=\\\"strong\\\">two</span> vowels, usually you pronounce the name  of the <span class=\\\"strong\\\">first</span>  vowel and the <span class=\\\"strong\\\">second</span> vowel is silent. <br/> Exceptions: <span class=\\\"italic\\\">could</span>, <span class=\\\"italic\\\">country</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> cheese<br/> treat<br/> fruit<br/> roast </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Rule 2\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" If a vowel is followed by  <span class=\\\"strong\\\">w</span> or  <span class=\\\"strong\\\">y</span>, you usually say the vowel with a  <span class=\\\"underline\\\">long vowel sound</span>.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> show<br/> brown<br/> pay<br/> blew  </span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Rule 3\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"If a word has <span class=\\\"strong\\\">one</span> vowel and it comes at the end of the word, you usually use a <span class=\\\"underline\\\">long vowel sound</span>.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" <span class=\\\"italic\\\">he<br/> me<br/> go<br/> try<span class=\\\"italic\\\">\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Rule 4 \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" If a word has a vowel and a consonant that is followed by <span class=\\\"strong\\\">e</span>, the <span class=\\\"strong\\\">e</span> is usually silent. However, it makes the vowel before it <span class=\\\"underline\\\">long</span>. <br/>Exceptions: <span class=\\\"italic\\\">gone</span>, <span class=\\\"italic\\\">have</span>, <span class=\\\"italic\\\">live</span>, <span class=\\\"italic\\\">come</span>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"italic\\\"> plane<br/> bone<br/> bake </span>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" instruction_table_background intro_table_border\"}", "type": "table"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "brie", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a type of soft cheese ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The baked brie appetizer sounds delicious.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le18ob04aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "in for a treat", "type": "name"}, {"content": "about to have something special ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(expression)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We are in for a treat tonight!", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le18ob04aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "lamb shanks ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a part of the lamb's leg that is made into a dish ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun) ", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: We are having lamb shanks for dinner.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn5le18ob04aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "cliff